UTME 2025 Chemistry SyllabusAim of the SyllabusThe aim of the Unified Tertiary Matriculation Examination (UTME) 2025 syllabus in Chemistry is to prepare candidates for the Board’s examination. It is designed to test their achievement of the following course objectives:(i) Understand the basic principles and concepts in chemistry;
(ii) Interpret scientific data relating to chemistry;
(iii) Deduce the relationships between chemistry and other sciences;
(iv) Apply the knowledge of chemistry to industry and everyday life.Syllabus Structure1. Separation of Mixtures and Purification of Chemical SubstancesTopics:
(a) Pure and impure substances
(b) Boiling and melting points
(c) Elements, compounds, and mixtures
(d) Chemical and physical changes
(e) Separation processes: evaporation, simple and fractional distillation, sublimation, filtration, crystallization, paper and column chromatography, simple and fractional crystallization, magnetization, decantationObjectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Distinguish between pure and impure substances;
(ii) Use boiling and melting points as criteria for purity of chemical substances;
(iii) Distinguish between elements, compounds, and mixtures;
(iv) Differentiate between chemical and physical changes;
(v) Identify the properties of the components of a mixture;
(vi) Specify the principle involved in each separation method;
(vii) Apply the basic principles of separation processes in everyday life.2. Chemical CombinationTopics:
Stoichiometry, laws of definite and multiple proportions, law of conservation of matter, Gay Lussac’s law of combining volumes, Avogadro’s law; chemical symbols, formulae, equations and their uses, relative atomic mass based on ¹²C=12, the mole concept and Avogadro’s number.Objectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Perform simple calculations involving formulae, equations/chemical composition and the mole concept;
(ii) Deduce the chemical laws from given expressions/statements/data;
(iii) Interpret graphical representations related to these laws;
(iv) Deduce the stoichiometry of chemical reactions.3. Kinetic Theory of Matter and Gas LawsTopics:
(a) An outline of the kinetic theory of matter:
   (i) Melting
   (ii) Vaporization
   (iii) Boiling
   (iv) Freezing
   (v) Condensation in terms of molecular motion and Brownian movement
(b) (i) The laws of Boyle, Charles, Graham, and Dalton (law of partial pressure); combined gas law, molar volume, and atomicity of gases
   (ii) The ideal gas equation (PV = nRT)
   (iii) The relationship between vapour density of gases and the relative molecular massObjectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Apply the theory to distinguish between solids, liquids, and gases;
(ii) Deduce reasons for change of state;
(iii) Draw inferences based on molecular motion;
(iv) Deduce gas laws from given expressions/statements;
(v) Interpret graphical representations related to these laws;
(vi) Perform simple calculations based on these laws, equations, and relationships.4. Atomic Structure and BondingTopics:
(a) (i) The concept of atoms, molecules, and ions; the works of Dalton, Millikan, Rutherford, Moseley, Thomson, and Bohr
   (ii) Atomic structure, electron configuration, atomic number, mass number, and isotopes (examples from atomic numbers 1 to 20)
   (iii) Shapes of s and p orbitals
(b) The periodic table and periodicity of elements; recognition of families (alkali metals, halogens, noble gases, transition metals); variation of properties: ionization energy, ionic radii, electron affinity, electronegativity
(c) Chemical bonding: electrovalency, covalency, coordinate bond, hydrogen bonding, metallic bonding, van der Waals’ forces
(d) Shapes of simple molecules: linear (H₂, O₂, Cl₂, HCl, CO₂), non-linear (H₂O), tetrahedral (CH₄), pyramidal (NH₃)
(e) Nuclear Chemistry:
   (i) Radioactivity – types and properties of radiations
   (ii) Nuclear reactions, simple equations, uses, and applications of natural and artificial radioactivityObjectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Distinguish between atoms, molecules, and ions;
(ii) Identify contributions of scientists to atomic structure;
(iii) Deduce protons, neutrons, and electrons from atomic/mass numbers;
(iv) Apply rules for electron arrangement;
(v) Identify common isotopic elements;
(vi) Relate isotopy to mass number; perform calculations;
(vii) Differentiate orbital shapes; determine electrons in s and p orbitals;
(viii) Relate atomic number to periodic table position and group properties;
(ix) Explain property variations across periods and down groups;
(x) Differentiate bonding types; deduce from electron configurations; relate to compound properties;
(xi) Differentiate molecular shapes;
(xii) Distinguish ordinary from nuclear reactions; natural vs. artificial radioactivity;
(xiii) Compare radiation properties; compute half-life; balance nuclear equations; identify applications.5. AirTopics:
(a) Natural gaseous constituents and proportions: nitrogen, oxygen, water vapour, carbon(IV) oxide, noble gases (argon, neon)
(b) Air as a mixture and uses of noble gasesObjectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Deduce reasons for air as a mixture;
(ii) Identify principles for separating air components;
(iii) Deduce reasons for compositional variation;
(iv) Specify uses of constituents.6. WaterTopics:
(a) Water as combustion product of hydrogen and its composition by volume
(b) Water as solvent; dissolved atmospheric gases and biological significance
(c) Hard and soft water: temporary/permanent hardness; softening methods
(d) Treatment of water for town supply
(e) Water of crystallization, efflorescence, deliquescence, hygrosropy; examples and usesObjectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Identify uses of water;
(ii) Identify effects of dissolved gases;
(iii) Distinguish hard/soft water; causes of hardness; removal methods;
(iv) Describe town water treatment processes;
(v) Distinguish phenomena; identify exhibiting compounds.7. SolubilityTopics:
(a) Unsaturated, saturated, supersaturated solutions; solubility curves; deductions (solubility in mole/dm³); calculations
(b) Solvents for fats, oils, paints; stain removal
(c) False solutions (suspensions, colloids): properties, examples (harmattan haze, fog, milk, aerosol, emulsion paints, rubber solution)Objectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Distinguish solution types;
(ii) Interpret solubility curves; calculate solute amounts; deduce temperature dependence;
(iii) Relate solvent nature to uses;
(iv) Differentiate true/false solutions; compare properties; provide examples.8. Environmental PollutionTopics:
(a) Sources and effects of pollutants
(b) Air pollution: H₂S, CO, SO₂, nitrogen oxides, CFCs, dust
(c) Water pollution: sewage, oil
(d) Soil pollution: oil spillage, biodegradable/non-biodegradable pollutantsObjectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Identify pollution types and pollutants;
(ii) Specify sources; classify biodegradable/non-biodegradable;
(iii) Specify environmental effects;
(iv) Identify control measures.9. Acids, Bases, and SaltsTopics:
(a) General characteristics/properties; indicators; acid basicity; salt types; acid definitions; examples (organic acids, alums); salt preparation (neutralization, precipitation, acid-metal); oxides and trioxocarbonate(IV) salts
(b) Conductance comparison of strong/weak acids/bases
(c) pH and pOH scale; calculations
(d) Acid/base titrations
(e) Salt hydrolysis: principles, examples (NH₄Cl, AlCl₃, Na₂CO₃, CH₃COONa)Objectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Distinguish acid/base properties; identify types; determine basicity; use indicators;
(ii) Identify salt preparation methods; classify salts;
(iii) Relate dissociation to strength/conductance;
(iv) Perform pH/pOH calculations; identify indicators; interpret titration curves; mole concept calculations;
(v) Balance hydrolysis equations; deduce solution properties.10. Oxidation and ReductionTopics:
(a) Oxidation: addition of oxygen/removal of hydrogen
(b) Reduction: removal of oxygen/addition of hydrogen
(c) In terms of electron transfer
(d) Oxidation numbers; balancing equations
(e) IUPAC nomenclature using oxidation numbers
(f) Tests for oxidizing/reducing agentsObjectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Identify oxidation/reduction expressions;
(ii) Classify reactions; balance redox equations;
(iii) Deduce oxidation numbers; compute electron transfer; identify redox species;
(iv) Distinguish agents; apply to naming; relate to abilities.11. ElectrolysisTopics:
(a) Electrolytes/non-electrolytes; Faraday’s laws
(b) (i) Electrolysis of dilute H₂SO₄, aqueous CuSO₄, CuCl₂, dilute/concentrated NaCl, fused NaCl
   (ii) Factors affecting ion discharge
(c) Uses: metal purification (Cu), element/compound production (Al, Na, O₂, Cl₂, NaOH)
(d) Electrochemical cells: redox series, half-cell reactions, electrode potentials (calculations)
(e) Corrosion as electrolytic process; prevention methodsObjectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Distinguish electrolytes/non-electrolytes;
(ii) Perform Faraday calculations;
(iii) Identify electrodes, reactions, products, factors;
(iv) Specify applications; identify cells; calculate potentials;
(v) Identify prevention methods.12. Energy ChangesTopics:
(a) ∆H in physical/chemical changes (dissolution, reactions); endothermic/exothermic
(b) Entropy as order-disorder; illustrations
(c) Spontaneity: ∆G criteriaObjectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Determine ∆H types; interpret graphs;
(ii) Relate state to orderliness;
(iii) Determine spontaneity conditions; relate ∆H, ∆S, ∆G;
(iv) Solve problems using ∆G = ∆H - T∆S.13. Rates of Chemical ReactionTopics:
(a) Factors affecting rate: temperature, concentration/pressure, surface area, catalyst (examples)
(b) Reaction rate curves
(c) Activation energy; Arrhenius’ law, collision theory; light effects (halogenation)Objectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Identify factors; determine effects of temperature, concentration, surface area, catalysts;
(ii) Moderate effects; interpret curves; solve problems; relate to kinetic theory;
(iii) Examine activation energy significance; deduce Ea from curves.14. Chemical EquilibriumTopics:
Reversible reactions; factors governing equilibrium; dynamic equilibrium; Le Chatelier’s principle; equilibrium constant (examples: steam on iron, N₂O₄ ⇌ 2NO₂). No calculations required.Objectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Identify factors affecting equilibrium position;
(ii) Predict factor effects;
(iii) Determine effects on equilibrium constant.15. Non-Metals and Their CompoundsTopics:
(a) Hydrogen: production, preparation, properties, uses, test
(b) Halogens (Cl₂ focus): preparation, properties, uses; HCl/HCl properties; chlorides test
(c) Oxygen and Sulphur:
   (i) Oxygen: preparation, properties, uses, production; oxides classification; ozone
   (ii) Sulphur: uses, allotropes; SO₂, H₂SO₄, H₂SO₃ properties/uses; H₂S properties; tests
(d) Nitrogen: preparation, production; NH₃ (Haber), properties, uses, salts; HNO₃; nitrates; oxides; nitrogen cycle
(e) Carbon: allotropes; CO₂, CO preparation/properties/uses; coal types/products; coke, synthetic gasObjectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Predict reagents, properties, uses, tests for gases/compounds;
(ii) Compare properties; specify tests for ions (Cl⁻, SO₄²⁻, SO₃²⁻, S²⁻, NH₄⁺, NO₃⁻, CO₃²⁻, HCO₃⁻);
(iii) Identify allotropes, significance, preparations, effects, products, uses as detailed.16. Metals and Their CompoundsTopics:
(a) General properties of metals
(b) Alkali metals (Na): NaOH, Na₂CO₃, NaHCO₃, NaCl
(c) Alkaline-earth metals (Ca): CaO, Ca(OH)₂, CaCO₃; cement, mortar; test for Ca²⁺
(d) Aluminium: purification, extraction, properties, uses; test for Al³⁺
(e) Tin: extraction, properties, uses
(f) First transition series: properties (configuration, oxidation states, complexes, coloured ions, catalysis)
(g) Iron: extraction, properties, uses, forms; test for Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺
(h) Copper: extraction, properties, uses; CuSO₄; test for Cu²⁺
(i) Alloys: steel, stainless steel, brass, bronze, type-metal, duralumin, soft solder, permalloy, alnico (constituents, uses)Objectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Specify properties, extraction methods, reactivities, uses, tests;
(ii) Determine production, reactivities, uses of compounds; cement composition;
(iii) Detail transition properties, IUPAC names, iron forms, copper preparation, alloy constituents/uses; compare alloys to pure metals.17. Organic CompoundsTopics:
Introduction to tetravalency, general formulae, IUPAC nomenclature, empirical formula determination.
(a) Aliphatic hydrocarbons: alkanes, alkenes, alkynes; petroleum, cracking, reforming
(b) Aromatic: benzene
(c) Alkanols: classes, production, reactions, Lucas test
(d) Alkanals/alkanones: distinguishing tests
(e) Alkanoic acids: reactions, examples
(f) Alkanoates: fats/oils, saponification, soap vs. detergent
(g) Amines: classes
(h) Carbohydrates: classification, tests, hydrolysis, uses
(i) Proteins: structure, hydrolysis, tests; enzymes
(j) Polymers: natural/synthetic, polymerization types, plasticsObjectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Derive names, relate to structure, catenation; classify by functional groups; derive formulae;
(ii) Relate structure to properties; derive isomers; distinguish isomerism; specify uses;
(iii) Detail hydrocarbons, petroleum fractions, processes, polymerization, vulcanization, tests;
(iv) Classify and detail alkanols, alkanals/alkanones, acids, alkanoates, amines, carbohydrates, proteins, polymers as specified.18. Chemistry and IndustryTopics:
Chemical industries: types, raw materials, relevancies; biotechnologyObjectives:
Candidates should be able to:
(i) Classify industries by products;
(ii) Identify raw materials; distinguish fine/heavy chemicals;
(iii) Enumerate relevance; relate processes to biotechnology.

